(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[24] = {"n":24,"set":"C","level":"Exam Standard","band":"B1 25% · B2 50% · B2+ 25%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"Dark matter","p":["When astronomers look at the universe, they see stars, galaxies, clouds of gas and dust, and countless other objects. It might seem natural to assume that this visible material makes up most of what exists. Yet according to modern cosmology, ordinary matter, the kind that forms stars, planets and people, accounts for only about five per cent of the universe. Roughly twenty-seven per cent appears to be an invisible substance known as dark matter, and the rest is an even more mysterious phenomenon called dark energy.","The first hints of dark matter came in the 1930s. The Swiss astronomer Fritz Zwicky studied a group of galaxies called the Coma Cluster and noticed that the galaxies were moving far too fast. Based on the visible material, there was not enough gravity to hold the cluster together; it should have flown apart. Zwicky suggested that some unseen mass must be present, which he called 'dark matter'. At the time, however, his idea received little attention.","Stronger evidence arrived in the 1970s from the work of the American astronomer Vera Rubin. She measured how fast stars orbit around the centres of spiral galaxies. According to the laws of gravity, stars far from the centre, where there is less visible matter, should move more slowly, just as the outer planets of our solar system orbit the Sun more slowly than the inner ones. Instead, Rubin found that outer stars moved almost as fast as those closer to the centre. The most likely explanation was that each galaxy is surrounded by a vast, invisible halo of dark matter.","Since then, further evidence has accumulated. Large masses bend the path of light passing near them, an effect predicted by Einstein called gravitational lensing. By studying how light from distant galaxies is distorted, astronomers can map where mass is located, and the maps reveal far more mass than can be seen. Measurements of the faint radiation left over from the early universe, known as the cosmic microwave background, also match predictions only if dark matter is included.","Despite all this evidence, nobody knows what dark matter actually is. It does not emit, absorb or reflect light, which is why it is called 'dark', and it appears to interact with ordinary matter only through gravity. Many physicists believe that it consists of an undiscovered type of particle. For decades, scientists have built extremely sensitive detectors, often placed deep underground to shield them from other radiation, hoping to catch a rare collision between a dark matter particle and an atom. So far, these experiments have not produced a convincing detection.","A minority of scientists have proposed a different solution: perhaps dark matter does not exist at all, and our understanding of gravity is incomplete. According to theories such as Modified Newtonian Dynamics, gravity may behave differently at very large distances. However, such theories have difficulty explaining all the observations, particularly gravitational lensing in colliding galaxy clusters. For now, dark matter remains one of the greatest unsolved puzzles in science: something that shapes the entire universe, yet has never been directly observed."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how stars are born","the evidence for dark matter and the mystery of what it is","the life of Albert Einstein","how telescopes are built"],"k":1,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage presents evidence and open questions.","h":"المادة المظلمة."},{"n":2,"s":"According to the passage, roughly how much of the universe is ordinary matter?","o":["about twenty-seven per cent","about half","almost all of it","about five per cent"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 1 states this.","h":"نحو خمسة بالمئة."},{"n":3,"s":"What did Zwicky notice about the Coma Cluster?","o":["Its galaxies were moving very slowly.","Its galaxies were moving too fast to be held together by visible matter.","It contained no stars.","It was getting smaller."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"سرعة المجرات كبيرة."},{"n":4,"s":"Why does the writer mention the planets of our solar system?","o":["to show that the Sun contains dark matter","to illustrate what Rubin expected to find","to explain how planets were formed","to compare their sizes with galaxies"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"Outer planets move more slowly.","h":"توضيح المتوقع."},{"n":5,"s":"The word 'accumulated' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["disappeared","been rejected","increased gradually","become confused"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"More evidence was gathered over time.","h":"تراكم."},{"n":6,"s":"What is gravitational lensing?","o":["a new type of telescope lens","the bending of light by large masses","the radiation from the early universe","the movement of stars around galaxies"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 defines it.","h":"انحناء الضوء."},{"n":7,"s":"Why are dark matter detectors often placed deep underground?","o":["to protect them from other kinds of radiation","because dark matter is found only underground","to keep them warm","to hide them from other scientists"],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"للحماية من الإشعاعات الأخرى."},{"n":8,"s":"In paragraph 5, the word 'it' in 'it appears to interact with ordinary matter' refers to","o":["dark matter","light","gravity","an atom"],"k":0,"ty":"reference","e":"Dark matter interacts only through gravity.","h":"تعود على المادة المظلمة."},{"n":9,"s":"What can be inferred about theories such as Modified Newtonian Dynamics?","o":["They have explained all observations.","They prove that dark matter exists.","They are supported by only a minority of scientists.","They were developed by Vera Rubin."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"'A minority of scientists' propose them.","h":"تدعمها أقلية."},{"n":10,"s":"Which statement best summarizes the passage?","o":["Dark matter has been directly observed.","Ordinary matter makes up most of the universe.","Scientists have stopped searching for dark matter.","Strong evidence suggests dark matter exists, but its nature remains unknown."],"k":3,"ty":"summary / conclusion","e":"It summarizes the main idea.","h":"دليل قوي وطبيعة مجهولة."}]},{"t":"The history of quarantine","p":["The recent global pandemic made the word 'quarantine' familiar to almost everyone. Yet the practice of separating people who may carry a disease is far older than modern medicine. Long before scientists understood germs, communities observed that some illnesses spread from person to person, and they tried to protect themselves by limiting contact with the sick. The history of these efforts reveals both how much our understanding of disease has changed and how some of the basic problems have remained remarkably similar.","Ancient texts from many cultures describe the isolation of people with infectious diseases. In the Islamic tradition, a well-known hadith advises that if people hear of a plague in a land, they should not enter it, and if it breaks out in a land where they are, they should not leave it. This principle, which remarkably anticipates modern quarantine rules, was applied during an outbreak in Syria in the time of Caliph Umar ibn al-Khattab. Medieval Muslim physicians such as Ibn Sina also wrote about the contagious nature of some diseases and recommended isolating patients for a period of forty days in certain cases.","The word 'quarantine' itself comes from Venice, the great trading city of medieval Italy. In the fourteenth century, Europe was devastated by the Black Death, a plague that killed perhaps a third of the continent's population. Because plague often arrived by ship, the nearby port of Ragusa, now Dubrovnik in Croatia, introduced a rule in 1377 requiring ships from infected areas to wait thirty days before their passengers and goods could enter. Venice later extended the period to forty days, quaranta giorni in Italian, which gave us the word 'quarantine'.","Over the following centuries, quarantine became a standard tool. Many ports built special buildings, called lazarettos, where travellers and cargo were held, often on islands away from the city. Ships arriving from regions with disease had to fly a yellow flag. These measures were not always effective, partly because people did not yet understand how diseases spread. Plague, for example, was carried mainly by fleas on rats, which could easily escape quarantine. Quarantine was also expensive and unpopular with merchants, who complained about delays and lost profits.","In the nineteenth century, scientific discoveries transformed the understanding of disease. Researchers identified the bacteria responsible for cholera, tuberculosis and other illnesses, and it became possible to set quarantine periods according to how long a disease took to appear after infection. International conferences, beginning in Paris in 1851, attempted to agree common rules, an effort that eventually contributed to the creation of the World Health Organization in 1948.","Today, quarantine remains a valuable public health measure, but it raises difficult questions. Separating healthy people who may have been exposed to a disease can slow its spread, yet it also restricts personal freedom and may cause economic hardship and psychological stress. For this reason, modern guidelines stress that quarantine should be based on scientific evidence, used only for as long as necessary, and accompanied by support such as food, medical care and clear communication. The long history of quarantine shows that balancing public safety with individual rights is not a new challenge."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which of the following is the best title for the passage?","o":["The Trade Routes of Venice","How Bacteria Were Discovered","The Life of Ibn Sina","Isolation Against Disease: Past and Present"],"k":3,"ty":"best title","e":"The passage traces quarantine through history.","h":"تاريخ الحجر الصحي."},{"n":12,"s":"What can be inferred from the first paragraph?","o":["Quarantine was invented during the recent pandemic.","People used isolation before they understood the causes of disease.","Germs were discovered in ancient times.","Early communities refused to limit contact."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"Communities isolated the sick long before germs were understood.","h":"استُخدم العزل قبل فهم الجراثيم."},{"n":13,"s":"Why does the writer describe the hadith as 'remarkably' anticipating modern rules?","o":["It described how bacteria spread.","It was written in Venice.","It required ships to wait forty days.","It recommended staying in or away from affected areas, like modern quarantine."],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"Its advice matches modern practice.","h":"تطابق المبدأ مع القواعد الحديثة."},{"n":14,"s":"Where does the word 'quarantine' come from?","o":["the name of a Croatian port","an Arabic medical term","a French conference","an Italian phrase meaning 'forty days'"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"أربعون يومًا بالإيطالية."},{"n":15,"s":"Which of these events happened FIRST?","o":["Ragusa introduced a thirty-day waiting rule.","Venice extended the period to forty days.","The first international conference met in Paris.","The World Health Organization was created."],"k":0,"ty":"sequence","e":"1377 is the earliest date of these.","h":"قاعدة راغوزا أولًا."},{"n":16,"s":"Why were early quarantine measures not always effective?","o":["Ships were not allowed to stop.","People did not understand how diseases spread.","Merchants always obeyed the rules.","Lazarettos were built in city centres."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Fleas on rats could escape quarantine.","h":"عدم فهم طرق الانتقال."},{"n":17,"s":"The word 'devastated' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["slightly affected","greatly improved","quickly abandoned","severely damaged"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"The plague killed a third of the population.","h":"دُمّر."},{"n":18,"s":"What made it possible to set quarantine periods scientifically?","o":["the building of lazarettos","the use of yellow flags","the identification of the bacteria that cause diseases","complaints from merchants"],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"اكتشاف البكتيريا."},{"n":19,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a drawback of quarantine today?","o":["restrictions on personal freedom","economic hardship","psychological stress","damage to the environment"],"k":3,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"The environment is not mentioned.","h":"البيئة غير مذكورة."},{"n":20,"s":"What is the writer's main conclusion?","o":["Quarantine should be abolished.","Quarantine always works perfectly.","Balancing public safety and individual rights has always been a challenge.","Only ancient methods are effective."],"k":2,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Stated in the final sentence.","h":"التوازن تحدٍ قديم."}]},{"t":"Desertification in the Sahel","p":["Stretching across Africa from Senegal in the west to Sudan and Eritrea in the east, the Sahel is a long belt of semi-arid land lying between the Sahara Desert to the north and the tropical savannas to the south. Its name comes from the Arabic word sahil, meaning 'shore' or 'coast', reflecting how travellers crossing the Sahara saw it as the edge of the sea of sand. More than a hundred million people live in the region, most of whom depend on farming and livestock for their living.","The Sahel has always had a challenging climate. Rain falls during a short wet season, and the amount varies greatly from year to year. In the late 1960s, 1970s and 1980s, the region suffered a series of severe droughts. Crops failed, millions of animals died and hundreds of thousands of people lost their lives in the resulting famines. Images of the disaster shocked the world and drew international attention to a process known as desertification.","Desertification does not mean that the Sahara is simply moving south like an advancing wave of sand, as was once commonly believed. Rather, it refers to the degradation of land in dry regions, so that soil loses its fertility and its ability to support plants. Desertification can occur in patches, far from the edge of any desert, and it is caused by a combination of climatic factors and human activities.","Several human activities contribute to the problem. As populations grow, farmers may cultivate land continuously, without allowing it to rest, so that nutrients are exhausted. Herders may keep more animals than the land can support, and overgrazing removes the vegetation that protects soil from wind and rain. Trees are cut for firewood and building materials. Once the ground is bare, the topsoil can be blown or washed away, and the land becomes increasingly difficult to restore.","In recent decades, however, the story has become more hopeful. Rainfall in parts of the Sahel has partly recovered since the 1990s, and satellite images have revealed a surprising 'greening' in some areas. Part of this improvement has come from the work of farmers themselves. In Niger, millions of farmers have adopted a technique called farmer-managed natural regeneration, in which they protect and prune tree shoots that grow from old stumps and roots in their fields. The trees provide shade, fodder and firewood, and their fallen leaves enrich the soil. Farmers in Burkina Faso have revived a traditional method called zaï, digging small pits that collect rainwater and organic matter around seeds.","Larger initiatives also exist. The Great Green Wall, launched by the African Union in 2007, aims to restore a hundred million hectares of degraded land across the region. Progress has been slower than hoped, and critics argue that simply planting trees is not enough unless local communities are involved and benefit directly. Saudi Arabia, which faces similar challenges, has launched its own Saudi Green Initiative, with plans to plant billions of trees and rehabilitate degraded land. The experience of the Sahel suggests that such projects are most likely to succeed when they combine scientific knowledge with the practical wisdom of the people who live on the land."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the history of the Sahara Desert","how to grow vegetables in deserts","the causes of land degradation in the Sahel and efforts to reverse it","the culture of people in Senegal"],"k":2,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage covers causes and solutions.","h":"التصحر في الساحل."},{"n":22,"s":"Why is the region called the Sahel?","o":["Travellers saw it as the 'shore' of the Sahara.","It lies beside the Atlantic Ocean.","It has many lakes and rivers.","It was named after a famous explorer."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 1 explains the name.","h":"الساحل = الشاطئ."},{"n":23,"s":"According to the passage, what is desertification?","o":["the movement of sand dunes into cities","a sudden increase in rainfall","the creation of new deserts by volcanoes","the degradation of land in dry regions"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 defines it.","h":"تدهور الأراضي."},{"n":24,"s":"What does the writer say about the idea that the Sahara is 'advancing like a wave'?","o":["It is supported by satellite images.","It is the main cause of famine.","It is a common but inaccurate belief.","It was proven in the 1980s."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"'as was once commonly believed'.","h":"اعتقاد خاطئ."},{"n":25,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a human cause of desertification?","o":["continuous cultivation","building large dams","overgrazing","cutting trees for firewood"],"k":1,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Dams are not mentioned.","h":"السدود غير مذكورة."},{"n":26,"s":"The word 'exhausted' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["increased","protected","used up completely","measured"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"The nutrients are used up.","h":"مُستنفد."},{"n":27,"s":"What is farmer-managed natural regeneration?","o":["protecting and pruning trees that grow from old roots and stumps","planting imported trees in rows","digging deep wells","moving animals to other regions"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 describes it.","h":"رعاية النباتات النامية من الجذور."},{"n":28,"s":"In paragraph 5, the word 'their' in 'their fallen leaves enrich the soil' refers to","o":["the farmers","the fields","the trees","the satellite images"],"k":2,"ty":"reference","e":"The trees' leaves enrich the soil.","h":"تعود على الأشجار."},{"n":29,"s":"What criticism of the Great Green Wall does the writer mention?","o":["It is too small to matter.","Planting trees is not enough without local involvement.","It has damaged farmland.","It has been completed too quickly."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 6 explains this.","h":"لا يكفي دون مشاركة السكان."},{"n":30,"s":"Why does the writer mention the Saudi Green Initiative?","o":["to criticize Saudi farming methods","to describe the history of the Sahel","to explain how zaï pits work","to show that similar efforts are taking place in Saudi Arabia"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"It links the topic to Saudi Arabia.","h":"ربط بمبادرة سعودية."}]},{"t":"Sign languages around the world","p":["Many hearing people assume that sign language is simply a way of spelling out spoken words with the hands, or that there is a single sign language used by deaf people everywhere. Both ideas are mistaken. Sign languages are complete, natural languages with their own vocabularies and grammars, and there are hundreds of them around the world, including American Sign Language, British Sign Language and Saudi Sign Language.","Sign languages are not based on the spoken languages of the countries where they are used. American Sign Language and British Sign Language, for example, are very different from each other, even though the hearing populations of the United States and Britain both speak English. In fact, American Sign Language is historically related to French Sign Language, because a French deaf teacher, Laurent Clerc, helped to establish the first permanent school for the deaf in the United States in 1817.","Like spoken languages, sign languages have complex grammar. Meaning is expressed not only through the shape and movement of the hands, but also through their location, the direction of movement and facial expressions. Raising the eyebrows, for instance, may turn a statement into a question, and the space around the signer's body can be used to represent people and places in a conversation. Once a person has been placed in a particular location, the signer can refer back to them simply by pointing to that space.","Some of the most fascinating evidence about the nature of language has come from sign languages. In the 1980s, a new school for deaf children opened in Nicaragua. Before then, most deaf people in the country had been isolated, communicating with their families through simple home signs. When the children came together, they rapidly created a shared system of communication. Remarkably, younger children who joined later made the system more complex and regular, adding grammatical features that the older children had not used. Within a few years, a fully developed new language, Nicaraguan Sign Language, had emerged. Linguists regard this as rare evidence of how human languages can be born.","Research has also shown that deaf children who learn a sign language from birth reach the same developmental milestones as hearing children learning a spoken language. They begin 'babbling' with their hands at around the same age as hearing babies babble with sounds, and they produce their first signs at about the same time as hearing children produce their first words. Brain imaging studies indicate that many of the same brain regions are involved in processing signed and spoken languages.","Despite these findings, sign languages have not always received the respect they deserve. For much of the twentieth century, many schools discouraged or even banned signing, insisting that deaf children should learn to lip-read and speak instead. Today, there is growing recognition of the value of sign languages. Many countries have given them official status, and in Saudi Arabia, efforts have been made to standardize Saudi Sign Language and to provide sign language interpretation on television and in public services. Learning even a few signs can be a meaningful way for hearing people to communicate with the deaf community."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how to become a sign language teacher","the history of French schools","the nature, variety and recognition of sign languages","the causes of deafness"],"k":2,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage covers these aspects.","h":"طبيعة لغات الإشارة."},{"n":32,"s":"Which of the following does the writer describe as a mistaken idea?","o":["There is one sign language used by all deaf people.","Sign languages have their own grammar.","There are hundreds of sign languages.","Saudi Arabia has its own sign language."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 1 rejects this idea.","h":"فكرة خاطئة."},{"n":33,"s":"Why is American Sign Language related to French Sign Language?","o":["Most Americans speak French.","Both countries use the same spoken language.","It was invented in France in 1980.","A French deaf teacher helped to establish deaf education in the United States."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"دور لوران كلير."},{"n":34,"s":"According to the passage, how can a statement become a question in a sign language?","o":["by raising the eyebrows","by signing more slowly","by repeating the sign","by spelling the words"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 gives this example.","h":"رفع الحاجبين."},{"n":35,"s":"In paragraph 3, the word 'them' in 'refer back to them simply by pointing' refers to","o":["the signer's hands","people placed in a location","facial expressions","questions"],"k":1,"ty":"reference","e":"The signer points to people placed in space.","h":"تعود على الأشخاص."},{"n":36,"s":"Why is the case of Nicaraguan Sign Language important to linguists?","o":["It shows how a new language can be born.","It was the first sign language ever used.","It was invented by teachers.","It is identical to Spanish."],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"'rare evidence of how human languages can be born'.","h":"نشأة لغة جديدة."},{"n":37,"s":"What can be inferred about the younger children in the Nicaraguan school?","o":["They were unable to learn the system.","They refused to use signs.","They played an important role in developing the language's grammar.","They learned the language from books."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"They made it more complex and regular.","h":"أسهموا في القواعد."},{"n":38,"s":"The word 'milestones' in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to","o":["important stages of development","medical tests","school examinations","physical injuries"],"k":0,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Stages such as babbling and first words.","h":"مراحل نمو."},{"n":39,"s":"What does brain imaging research suggest?","o":["Signed and spoken languages use many of the same brain regions.","Sign languages use no part of the brain.","Deaf children develop language more slowly.","Spoken languages are more complex."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"مناطق دماغية مشتركة."},{"n":40,"s":"What is the writer's attitude towards sign languages?","o":["doubtful about their value","respectful and supportive","critical of deaf schools today","uninterested"],"k":1,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"The writer stresses their value.","h":"تقدير ودعم."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"On no account ______ the laboratory without wearing safety glasses.","o":["should you enter","you should enter","you enter","entering"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"should you enter — On no account at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد On no account."},{"n":42,"s":"If the government had invested in public transport earlier, traffic ______ so bad today.","o":["wouldn't be","wouldn't have been","won't be","isn't"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"wouldn't be — Mixed conditional: past cause, present result.","h":"شرط مختلط."},{"n":43,"s":"______ in 1957, King Saud University is the oldest university in the Kingdom.","o":["Founding","Founded","Having founded","It was founded"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Founded — Past participle clause (passive).","h":"اسم المفعول."},{"n":44,"s":"The doctor recommended that he ______ his intake of sugar.","o":["reduces","reducing","will reduce","reduce"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"reduce — Subjunctive after recommend that.","h":"صيغة الطلب."},{"n":45,"s":"The ancient manuscript is thought ______ in the twelfth century.","o":["to have written","to be writing","to have been written","having been written"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to have been written — Passive reporting about the past.","h":"is thought to have been."},{"n":46,"s":"If only I ______ the answer during the interview yesterday!","o":["knew","had known","would know","have known"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had known — If only + past perfect for past regret.","h":"If only."},{"n":47,"s":"It was the lack of funding, not the lack of interest, ______ caused the project to fail.","o":["what","that","who","where"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"that — Cleft sentence: It was … that.","h":"جملة التوكيد."},{"n":48,"s":"Under no circumstances ______ personal information to strangers online.","o":["should you give","you should give","you give","give you"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"should you give — Negative adverbial: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Under no circumstances."},{"n":49,"s":"The researchers interviewed fifty farmers, all of ______ had experienced drought.","o":["who","them","whom","which"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"whom — all of whom.","h":"all of whom."},{"n":50,"s":"______ you need any further information, please contact our office.","o":["Would","Had","Should","Were"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Should — Should for an inverted first conditional.","h":"Should بدل If."},{"n":51,"s":"By the time the ambulance arrived, the man ______ consciousness.","o":["has regained","regains","was regained","had regained"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had regained — Past perfect before a past event.","h":"الماضي التام."},{"n":52,"s":"The minister refused ______ any questions from journalists.","o":["answering","answer","to answer","for answering"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to answer — refuse + to-infinitive.","h":"refuse to."},{"n":53,"s":"She objected ______ to work on Fridays.","o":["to ask","to being asked","for asking","being asked"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to being asked — object to + passive gerund.","h":"object to + -ing."},{"n":54,"s":"______ hard she tries, she can never finish the crossword.","o":["Although","Whatever","However","Despite"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"However — However + adjective/adverb.","h":"However + صفة."},{"n":55,"s":"I'd sooner you ______ the truth now than later.","o":["tell me","told me","will tell me","telling me"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"told me — would sooner + subject + past.","h":"would sooner."},{"n":56,"s":"The main bridge ______ at the moment, so we have to use the detour.","o":["is being repaired","is repairing","repairs","has repaired"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"is being repaired — Present continuous passive.","h":"المبني للمجهول المستمر."},{"n":57,"s":"______ his age, he is remarkably fit.","o":["Considered","Consider","To consider","Considering"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Considering — Considering = taking into account.","h":"Considering."},{"n":58,"s":"The professor encouraged us ______ questions during the lecture.","o":["asking","ask","that ask","to ask"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to ask — encourage + object + to-infinitive.","h":"encourage sb to."},{"n":59,"s":"The faster the internet connection, ______ the download.","o":["the quicker","quicker","the quickest","more quick"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"the quicker — Double comparative.","h":"the … the."},{"n":60,"s":"You may borrow my notes ______ you return them by Sunday.","o":["unless","even if","in case","as long as"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"as long as — as long as = on condition that.","h":"as long as."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"The (A) police (B) has (C) arrested the (D) suspect.","o":["police","has","arrested","suspect"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"has — police takes a plural verb: have.","h":"police جمع."},{"n":62,"s":"She (A) explained the (B) problem (C) me (D) clearly.","o":["explained","problem","me","clearly"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"me — explain something to someone: to me.","h":"explain to."},{"n":63,"s":"(A) Having (B) read the instructions, the machine (C) was easy to (D) use.","o":["Having","read","was","use"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Having — Dangling participle: the machine did not read. Since I had read …","h":"فاعل غير منطقي."},{"n":64,"s":"Nobody (A) could believe the news, (B) which (C) was so (D) surprised.","o":["could","which","was","surprised"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"surprised — -ing adjective for the cause: surprising.","h":"surprising."},{"n":65,"s":"If (A) I (B) were you, I (C) will (D) apologize.","o":["I","were","will","apologize"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"will — Second conditional: would.","h":"would."},{"n":66,"s":"He is one of (A) the (B) more talented (C) players in (D) the team.","o":["the","more","players","the"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"more — Superlative needed: most.","h":"التفضيل most."},{"n":67,"s":"The book (A) that I (B) borrowed from the library (C) is overdue, so I must (D) returning it today.","o":["that","borrowed","is","returning"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"returning — must + base verb: return.","h":"must + المصدر."},{"n":68,"s":"(A) Who's notebook is this? It (B) was left (C) on the (D) desk.","o":["Who's","was","on","desk"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Who's — Possession: Whose.","h":"Whose للملكية."},{"n":69,"s":"We (A) are looking forward (B) to (C) meet you (D) soon.","o":["are","to","meet","soon"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"meet — look forward to + -ing: meeting.","h":"to + -ing."},{"n":70,"s":"There (A) are (B) much people (C) waiting (D) outside.","o":["are","much","waiting","outside"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"much — people is countable: many.","h":"many مع المعدود."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M24-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"How does the woman feel about her new job?","o":["She dislikes everything about it.","She wants to leave immediately.","She enjoys the work but finds the travel tiring.","She finds the work boring."],"k":2,"ty":"opinion","e":"'it's a bit of both'.","h":"مشاعر مختلطة."}],"t":"Mixed feelings about a new job","sc":["Situation: Two friends are talking in a café. (73 words)","Woman: So, how's the new job going? It's been a month now, hasn't it?","Woman: Honestly, it's a bit of both. The work itself is fascinating, and I'm learning so much. But the commute is exhausting. It takes me over an hour each way.","Woman: That's a long time. Is there any possibility of working from home sometimes?","Woman: My manager said we could discuss it after my probation period. So I'm hoping things will get easier."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M24-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"What is Saud hinting at?","o":["He was ill at the weekend.","His friend forgot his birthday.","His phone was broken.","He wants to change his lunch plans."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"He mentions turning twenty-five.","h":"نسيان عيد ميلاده."}],"t":"A friend's hint about forgetting a birthday","sc":["Situation: Two friends are talking on the phone. (57 words)","Man: Hi, Saud. How was your weekend?","Man: Quiet. I spent most of Saturday waiting for messages that never came. It's not every day a person turns twenty-five, you know.","Man: Twenty-five? Oh no, your birthday was on Saturday! I'm so sorry. I completely forgot.","Man: Don't worry about it. You can make it up to me with lunch this week."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M24-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"What is the tutor's main criticism?","o":["The research was weak.","The slides contained too much text.","The presentation was too short.","Lama spoke too quietly."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"She suggests fewer words per slide.","h":"النصوص كثيرة."}],"t":"A tutor's polite criticism","sc":["Situation: A tutor speaks to a student about her presentation. (76 words)","Woman: Thank you, Lama. That was a well-researched presentation. I wonder, though, whether the audience might have followed it more easily with fewer words on each slide.","Woman: Do you think the slides were too crowded?","Woman: Some of them, yes. When there's a lot of text, people tend to read instead of listening to you. Perhaps keep the slides for key points and images, and say the rest.","Woman: That makes sense. I'll simplify them before the final presentation."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M24-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"What does the second man mean by his question?","o":["He does not want to cover the shift again.","He has forgotten the date.","He wants to attend the wedding.","He is happy to work on Saturday."],"k":0,"ty":"function","e":"He reminds his colleague he has already helped twice.","h":"رفض غير مباشر."}],"t":"A refusal disguised as a question","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are talking at work. (53 words)","Man: Could you cover my shift on Saturday? I've got a family wedding.","Man: This Saturday? Haven't I covered your last two Saturday shifts already?","Man: Well, yes, you have. I'm sorry, I didn't realize I'd asked so often.","Man: It's fine, but maybe ask Turki this time. I really need a weekend with my family too."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M24-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"What does the woman mean when she says she is 'up to my ears in work'?","o":["She has finished all her work.","She has problems with her hearing.","She is extremely busy.","She finds the work easy."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"She lists essays and an exam.","h":"مشغولة جدًا."}],"t":"An idiom about a busy week","sc":["Situation: Two students are talking in the library. (54 words)","Woman: Are you coming to the book club on Wednesday?","Woman: I wish I could, but I'm up to my ears in work this week. I've got two essays due and a chemistry exam on Thursday.","Woman: That sounds stressful. Good luck with everything.","Woman: Thanks. I'll definitely come to the next meeting, once this week is over."]},{"rec":6,"a":"M24-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"How does the man feel about driving to Abha?","o":["He agrees, but not very enthusiastically.","He is very excited about it.","He refuses completely.","He prefers to take the bus."],"k":0,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"'Well … I suppose' shows reluctance.","h":"موافقة على مضض."}],"t":"A reluctant agreement to a plan","sc":["Situation: A husband and wife are discussing a holiday. (64 words)","Woman: I think we should drive to Abha for the holiday instead of flying. We could stop in Al-Baha on the way.","Man: Drive? It's more than ten hours.","Woman: But we'd see the mountains, and we'd have the car when we get there.","Man: Well … I suppose it could be interesting. All right, we'll drive, but we're stopping every two hours, and you're doing half the driving."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M24-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"What happened to the second section?","o":["It was moved to another document.","It was deleted by mistake.","It was never written.","It was sent to the manager."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"'I moved it to a separate document'.","h":"نُقل إلى ملف آخر."}],"t":"A misunderstanding about a shared file","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are talking about a document. (73 words)","Man: Did you delete the second section of our report? It's gone from the shared file.","Man: No, I didn't delete it. I moved it to a separate document because the file was getting too large to open on my phone.","Man: Oh, I see. I thought we'd lost two days of work.","Man: Sorry, I should have told you. I'll put a link at the top of the main file so we can both find it."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M24-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"How does the man react to the news?","o":["He is annoyed.","He is worried.","He is disappointed.","He is surprised. Longer conversations"],"k":3,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"'Really? I didn't expect that.'","h":"متفاجئ."}],"t":"An unexpected compliment","sc":["Situation: A manager speaks to an employee. (67 words)","Woman: Majid, do you have a minute?","Man: Of course. Is something wrong?","Woman: Not at all. I just wanted to say that the client you helped last week called to thank us. She said you were the most patient and helpful person she'd ever dealt with.","Man: Really? I didn't expect that. I was only doing my job.","Woman: Well, you did it very well. I've mentioned it to the director."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M24-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"Why does the second student want to begin with definitions?","o":["because the judges require it","to shape the debate by making vague terms clear","to save time for questions","to confuse the other team"],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"'if we define them carefully, we can shape the whole debate'.","h":"تحديد المصطلحات."},{"n":80,"s":"How do they plan to respond to arguments about mental health?","o":["deny that the problems exist","ignore the arguments completely","agree that social media should be banned","accept the problems but argue that they come from how platforms are used"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'We shouldn't deny those problems.'","h":"الاعتراف ثم إعادة التفسير."}],"t":"Discussing a debate strategy","sc":["Situation: Two students are preparing for a university debate. (198 words)","Man: Our motion is that social media does more harm than good to teenagers, and we're on the opposing side. How should we start?","Man: I think we should open with a clear definition. 'Harm' and 'good' are vague, so if we define them carefully, we can shape the whole debate.","Man: Good idea. Then our first argument could be about education: students use social media to share resources and join study groups.","Man: And the second could be about social connection, especially for teenagers who feel isolated in their schools.","Man: We also need to predict their arguments. They'll probably talk about mental health and cyberbullying.","Man: Right. We shouldn't deny those problems. That would make us look unreasonable. Instead, we can argue that they're caused by how platforms are used, not by social media itself, and that education and better regulation can reduce them.","Man: That's a strong approach. Who'll give the closing speech?","Man: You should. You're better at speaking without notes. Just remember to summarize our points rather than introduce new ones.","Man: Okay. Let's practise with a timer tomorrow. Each speech has to be under four minutes.","Man: And let's ask Fahad to play the other team, so we can practise our responses."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M24-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"Why do they plan to collect samples at least three days after rain?","o":["because the roads are closed after rain","because the kits do not work in rain","because the forecast is unreliable","so the soil is not too wet"],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"Dr. Rania's advice.","h":"حتى لا تكون التربة رطبة."},{"n":82,"s":"Why does the first woman stress labelling bags immediately?","o":["The technician requires it.","Another group once mixed up their samples.","Labels are expensive.","The GPS device does not work."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"A group had to repeat the work.","h":"تجنب خلط العينات."}],"t":"Planning a fieldwork schedule","sc":["Situation: Two geography students are planning fieldwork. (187 words)","Woman: We have to collect soil samples from five sites around Taif for our project. How should we organize the trips?","Woman: The three sites in Al-Hada are close together, so we could do them in one day. The other two are south of the city.","Woman: Al-Hada first, then. When should we go?","Woman: Dr. Rania said samples should be collected at least three days after rain, so the soil isn't too wet. The forecast says it might rain on Monday.","Woman: Then let's aim for Thursday for Al-Hada and the following Sunday for the southern sites.","Woman: We'll need to borrow the soil testing kit from the department. Only two kits are available, so we should book them now.","Woman: I'll email the technician this afternoon. What else do we need?","Woman: Sample bags, labels, a GPS device to record the exact locations, and plenty of water. It gets hot by midday, so we should start early, around seven.","Woman: And we must label every bag immediately. Last year, a group mixed up their samples and had to repeat the whole thing.","Woman: Good point. I'll prepare the labels in advance with the site numbers."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M24-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"What was the main weakness of the report, according to the tutor?","o":["Its parts did not fit together well.","Its research was not thorough.","Its recommendations were impractical.","It was submitted late."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"It read like three separate parts.","h":"ضعف الترابط."},{"n":84,"s":"What will Omar do next time?","o":["write all the sections","choose the topic","check the references only","edit the whole report Talks and lectures"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'I'd be happy to do the editing.'","h":"تحرير التقرير."}],"t":"Reviewing feedback on a group project","sc":["Situation: Three students are reading their tutor's feedback. (187 words)","Omar: We got seventy-four per cent. Not bad, but I hoped for more.","Yasser: Let's look at the comments. She says our research was thorough and our recommendations were practical.","Bandar: So where did we lose marks?","Yasser: Mainly on structure. She says the report reads like three separate parts written by three different people.","Omar: Well, that's exactly what it was. We each wrote a section and just put them together the night before.","Bandar: She also mentions inconsistent referencing. I used one style, and you two used another.","Yasser: Those are easy problems to fix. Next time, let's agree on a referencing style at the start and leave at least two days for one person to edit the whole report.","Omar: I'd be happy to do the editing. I enjoy that kind of work.","Bandar: Great. And maybe we should meet halfway through to check that our sections connect logically.","Yasser: Agreed. If we fix the structure, we could easily get over eighty next time.","Omar: Should we also ask her for a meeting to discuss the feedback in more detail?","Bandar: Good idea. Her office hours are on Monday afternoon. I'll send her an email tonight."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M24-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"Why did the pearling season take place in summer?","o":["Oysters only grow in summer.","Merchants travelled in winter.","The water was warm enough for long periods of diving.","The sea was calmer in winter."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"الماء دافئ."},{"n":86,"s":"What does the lecturer say about divers and debt?","o":["Divers were paid in advance and never in debt.","Many divers owed money that carried over from year to year.","Debts were cancelled after every season.","Only captains had debts."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"The debt carried over if the catch was poor.","h":"دين مستمر."},{"n":87,"s":"Which two reasons does the lecturer give for the collapse of pearling?","o":["dangerous storms and lack of boats","new laws and a shortage of divers","pollution and the discovery of oil","lower demand and the production of cultured pearls"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"The depression and Japanese cultured pearls.","h":"تراجع الطلب واللؤلؤ المستزرع."}],"t":"Extended lecture: how pearl diving shaped Gulf towns","sc":["Situation: A history lecturer speaks to students. (302 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Before the discovery of oil, one of the most important economic activities along the Arabian Gulf was pearl diving. For centuries, the shallow waters of the Gulf, particularly around Bahrain, Qatar, the eastern coast of Arabia and what is now the United Arab Emirates, contained some of the richest pearl beds in the world. Gulf pearls were highly valued and traded as far away as India and Europe. The pearling season, known as al-ghous, lasted about four months during the hot summer, when the water was warm enough for long periods of diving. Hundreds of boats would leave the coastal towns, each carrying a crew of divers and pullers. The divers descended repeatedly to depths of ten or fifteen metres, holding their breath for a minute or more, with a stone tied to their feet to help them sink and a clip on their nose. They collected oysters in a basket, then tugged a rope as a signal for the pullers to haul them up. The work was dangerous and exhausting, and most divers were trapped in a system of debt. Boat captains and merchants advanced money to divers' families before the season, and if the catch was poor, the debt carried over to the next year. Pearling shaped the social and physical development of Gulf towns. Settlements such as Darin on Tarout Island and Al-Jubail grew as pearling centres, and wealthy merchants built large houses and mosques. Songs, traditions and vocabulary connected with the sea remain part of Gulf culture today. The industry collapsed in the 1930s for two main reasons: the global economic depression reduced demand for luxury goods, and Japanese scientists perfected methods of producing cultured pearls cheaply. Fortunately for the region, the decline of pearling coincided with the discovery of oil, which transformed the Gulf economies."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M24-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"Why do experienced professionals often make good quick decisions?","o":["They always use slow analytical thinking.","They are never under stress.","They recognize patterns from previous situations.","They follow orders without thinking."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"يتعرفون على الأنماط."},{"n":89,"s":"What is 'tunnel vision' in this context?","o":["focusing on too little information and ignoring alternatives","the ability to see clearly at night","making decisions very slowly","asking a team for advice"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Defined in the lecture.","h":"التركيز الضيق."},{"n":90,"s":"Why does the lecturer mention surgical safety checklists?","o":["to criticize hospitals","to show that checklists waste time","as an example of a tool that reduced errors","to explain how pilots are trained"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"They reduced complications.","h":"مثال على أداة تقلل الأخطاء."}],"t":"Lecture: the psychology of decision-making under pressure","sc":["Situation: A psychology lecturer speaks to students. (292 words)","Lecturer: Good afternoon. Today we'll explore how people make decisions under pressure, whether that pressure comes from time limits, high stakes or stress. Psychologists often describe two systems of thinking. The first is fast, automatic and intuitive: it allows us to react quickly, for example when we brake suddenly to avoid an accident. The second is slow, deliberate and analytical: it's what we use when we compare options carefully or solve a complex problem. Under pressure, people tend to rely more on the fast system. This can be helpful when the situation is familiar. Research on firefighters, pilots and emergency doctors shows that experienced professionals often make excellent rapid decisions, because they recognize patterns from previous situations and know almost immediately what to do. However, in unfamiliar situations, relying on intuition can lead to errors. Under stress, people also tend to focus narrowly on a few pieces of information, ignoring alternatives, a phenomenon sometimes called tunnel vision. They may also stick with their first decision even when new evidence suggests it is wrong. So how can decision-making under pressure be improved? First, training matters. Repeated practice in realistic simulations helps people build the experience that makes intuition reliable. That's why pilots spend many hours in flight simulators. Second, simple checklists can prevent important steps from being forgotten. Hospitals that introduced surgical safety checklists, for example, reduced complications significantly. Third, teams can be encouraged to speak up. In many accidents, a junior team member noticed a problem but felt unable to challenge a senior colleague. Finally, when possible, taking even a short pause, just a few seconds to breathe and reassess, can help people shift from purely automatic reactions to more careful thought. Pressure cannot always be avoided, but its effects can be managed."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Some people prefer driving.","Making cities more walkable clearly benefits both people and the environment.","Shops open at different times.","Pavements are made of concrete."],"x":["Many cities are creating more space for pedestrians. Wide pavements, shaded walkways and car-free streets encourage people to walk, which improves their health and supports local shops. Fewer cars also mean less noise and pollution. ______"],"k":1,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"It sums up the benefits.","h":"خلاصة الفوائد."},{"n":92,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"x":["(1) The Hejaz Railway was built in the early twentieth century. (2) It connected Damascus with Madinah. (3) It greatly reduced the travel time for pilgrims. (4) Modern trains can travel at very high speeds. (5) However, parts of the line were damaged during the First World War."],"k":2,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Modern train speeds do not belong in a paragraph about the Hejaz Railway.","h":"القطارات الحديثة خارج الفكرة."},{"n":93,"s":"(A) Despite of the high cost, many families (B) choose private (C) schools for their (D) children.","o":["Despite of","choose","schools","children"],"k":0,"ty":"error","e":"Despite takes no 'of'.","h":"Despite بدون of."},{"n":94,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Libraries are usually quiet.","Many students drink coffee.","Exams are stressful.","People have very different study habits."],"x":["______ Some prefer to study early in the morning, while others concentrate better at night. Some need complete silence, while others work well with background noise."],"k":3,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details describe different habits.","h":"اختلاف عادات المذاكرة."},{"n":95,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Clothes come in many sizes.","Some shops are very large.","Consumers may need to consider the true cost of cheap clothing before buying.","Cotton grows in hot countries."],"x":["Fast fashion allows people to buy fashionable clothes at very low prices. However, producing huge quantities of cheap clothing uses enormous amounts of water and energy, and many items are thrown away after being worn only a few times. ______"],"k":2,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"It draws a conclusion from the problem.","h":"خلاصة منطقية."},{"n":96,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 1","Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4"],"x":["(1) Bees play a vital role in agriculture. (2) They pollinate many of the crops that humans eat. (3) Without them, the production of fruits and nuts would fall sharply. (4) Honey has been used as medicine for centuries. (5) Unfortunately, bee populations are declining in many parts of the world."],"k":3,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Honey as medicine does not develop the point about agriculture.","h":"العسل كدواء خارج الفكرة."},{"n":97,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["3 – 2 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 1 – 4","2 – 1 – 3 – 4","2 – 3 – 4 – 1"],"x":["(1) However, the tickets had already sold out.","(2) Faisal wanted to watch the final match at the stadium.","(3) He checked the website as soon as it opened.","(4) Luckily, his cousin had a spare ticket and invited him."],"k":1,"ty":"best order","e":"goal → action → problem → solution.","h":"هدف ثم محاولة ثم مشكلة ثم حل."},{"n":98,"s":"The (A) number of cars on the road (B) has increased, (C) causing more (D) pollutions.","o":["number","has","causing","pollutions"],"k":3,"ty":"error","e":"pollution is uncountable.","h":"pollution غير معدود."},{"n":99,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Without fixed school hours, they must decide for themselves when to study.","Universities have large libraries.","Some courses start in January.","Students often live in dormitories."],"x":["Many students find it hard to manage their time at university. ______ Planning each week in advance and setting small goals can help them stay on track."],"k":0,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"It explains the difficulty before the advice.","h":"تفسير الصعوبة."},{"n":100,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["Nevertheless","Therefore","Similarly","In addition Teacher Section — Mock 24"],"x":["The experiment did not produce the expected results. ______, the researchers learned a great deal from their mistakes."],"k":0,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"The second idea contrasts with the first.","h":"Nevertheless للتضاد."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
